"""The four original foundations workbenches, kept with stable controls."""
def labs(n):
    common='<span class="eyebrow">TRY IT · INTERACTIVE MODEL</span>'
    if n==1:
        return '<section class="lab" id="crash-lab">'+common+'''<h3>What survives a restart?</h3><p>Choose when the application reports success. Use “Next event” to follow the request, then restart the process and check whether the task remains.</p><label>When to report success <select id="crash-policy"><option value="memory">A · memory only</option><option value="early">B · report success before commit</option><option value="durable" selected>C · commit before reporting success</option></select></label><div class="lab-actions"><button id="crash-next" type="button">Next event →</button><button id="crash-restart" type="button">Restart process</button><button id="crash-reset" class="quiet" type="button">Reset</button></div><div class="lab-state" id="crash-state" aria-live="polite"></div><ol id="crash-trace" class="event-trace"></ol><p class="assumption">This model follows one request. A commit either finishes completely or does not happen. Restart removes memory but preserves committed storage. No real files are written.</p><noscript><p>Without JavaScript: memory-only tasks disappear after restart. Reporting success before commit can lose a task the user was told was saved. Committing first preserves it under this model.</p></noscript></section>'''
    if n==2:
        return '<section class="lab" id="change-lab">'+common+'''<h3>Where would you make the change?</h3><p>Choose a change and compare the parts that need editing in the two designs.</p><label>Change scenario <select id="change-scenario"><option value="format">Replace JSON input with a form</option><option value="rule">Change the task title limit</option><option value="schema">Rename a storage column</option><option value="retry">Recognise repeated create requests</option></select></label><div id="change-result" aria-live="polite"></div><p class="assumption">The results follow our example’s division of responsibilities. They show where edits are needed, without estimating how long those edits take.</p><noscript><p>The adapter handles input format, the service checks titles, and the repository handles the database layout. Recognising retries needs agreement across all three.</p></noscript></section>'''
    if n==3:
        return '<section class="lab" id="decision-lab">'+common+'''<h3>Which preferences matter most?</h3><div class="lab-controls"><label>Simplicity weight <input type="range" id="weight-simple" min="0" max="5" value="5"><output for="weight-simple" id="out-simple">5</output></label><label>Growth weight <input type="range" id="weight-growth" min="0" max="5" value="1"><output for="weight-growth" id="out-growth">1</output></label><label>Separate storage weight <input type="range" id="weight-independent" min="0" max="5" value="1"><output for="weight-independent" id="out-independent">1</output></label></div><div id="decision-result" aria-live="polite"></div><p class="assumption">The scores come from Table 1.3.2. A higher weight gives that preference more influence. Every acceptable choice must still preserve tasks across a process restart.</p><noscript><p>At the initial weights, the embedded-store score is 4.00 and the separate-database score is 2.57. Memory alone cannot keep tasks after a process restart.</p></noscript></section>'''
    return '<section class="lab" id="view-lab">'+common+'''<h3>Zoom in on the same application</h3><div class="view-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Architecture view"><button type="button" data-view="context" aria-pressed="true">Context</button><button type="button" data-view="container" aria-pressed="false">Containers</button><button type="button" data-view="component" aria-pressed="false">Components</button><button type="button" data-view="code" aria-pressed="false">Code</button></div><div id="view-result" aria-live="polite"></div><p class="assumption">Select a level to see which parts it shows. Figure 1.4.1 and Table 1.4.1 explain all four levels without using these controls.</p></section>'''

